    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Mizerka (Polish Trick-Taking)',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Mizerka is a popular Polish trick-taking game for three players, played with a standard 52-card deck. It is a game of 'Contracts', where each player must eventually choose to play through six distinct game types (four suits, No Trump, and the eponymous 'Mizerka'â€”a misere avoidance contract). It features a unique 'Partial Deal' bidding phase and a talon exchange that allows players to refine their hands. It is highly strategic, requiring players to balance their aggressive and defensive contracts throughout 18 total rounds.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Rooted in the Slavic tradition of trick-taking games like preference and thousand (1000). It became a standardized parlor game in Poland during the 20th century.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Three players. Play is clockwise. Any player may deal first; the turn to deal passes clockwise. In each deal the three roles are: dealer (deals and has a quota of 1 trick in trump contracts, 7 in mizerka), forehand or left of dealer (chooses the contract after seeing six cards and has quota 7 in trump contracts, 1 in mizerka), and middlehand or right of dealer (quota 5 in both). The player to dealer's left leads to the first trick.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '52 cards. Rank: Ace (High) to 2 (Low).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '52 cards. Rank: Ace (High) to 2 (Low).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Each player gets 6 cards. 6 for the talon. Then the rest (13 total each + 13 for talon).',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Standard (six contracts): Six contracts: clubs, hearts, spades, diamonds, no trumps, and mizerka (misère). Each player chooses each contract once over 18 deals.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: "Contract order: Contract is chosen by player to dealer's left after seeing first six cards; no other variant specified in source.",
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Talon exchange: Left of dealer exchanges first, then right of dealer, then dealer if talon remains; no obligation to discard.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
    description: "Mizerka is a popular Polish trick-taking game for three players, played with a standard 52-card deck. It is a game of 'Contracts', where each player must eventually choose to play through six distinct game types (four suits, No Trump, and the eponymous 'Mizerka'â€”a misere avoidance contract). It features a unique 'Partial Deal' bidding phase and a talon exchange that allows players to refine their hands. It is highly strategic, requiring players to balance their aggressive and defensive contracts throughout 18 total rounds.",
